+# Methodology
+
+What a good workout is, for the planner to weigh. Written by a human (TRAINER.md, *Stores*); the agent reads it and never edits it.
+
+**Status: draft.** Every number below is a placeholder until the user confirms it. Sources were cited from memory and have not been checked against the papers yet.
+
+Everything here is a criterion, not a rule. The validator checks legality (ADR-0010); nothing in this file is checked by code, and none of it may become a constant (ADR-0001).
+
+## Words
+
+| term | meaning here |
+|---|---|
+| **working set** | one round of one exercise taken close to failure: about 0-3 reps in reserve. Warm-up rounds are not working sets. |
+| **hard sets per muscle** | working sets whose exercise has that muscle as primary, counted per cycle. |
+| **variant** | an exercise that trains the same pattern and the same primary muscles as another in the plan (e.g. two push-up angles). |
+
+## How much
+
+| question | guidance | source |
+|---|---|---|
+| hard sets per muscle per cycle | about **10-20** for growth; about **4-6** keeps what is there | [1], [2] |
+| hard sets per muscle per workout | about **6-10**; past that, more sets add little and cost time | [1] |
+| exercises per group per workout | small group (arms, shoulders, core): **1 per muscle**. Large group (legs, back, chest): **2-3**, on different patterns | — |
+| working sets per exercise | **2-4** | [3] |
+| reps | **5-30** all grow muscle when the set ends near failure; under 6 for strength | [4] |
+| variants in one workout | at most **one** besides the original, and only when the group has nothing else admissible | — |
+
+**Bodyweight and bands.** Load cannot be added, so a working set is set by reps in reserve, not by a rep count. 8 reps of a push-up the user can do 25 of is not a working set. Prefer the harder variant, a slower tempo or a longer range over adding a second variant [4].
+
+> **Yours:** the volume band for your goal, and whether a cycle of N workouts should be read as a week.
+
+## Rest
+
+| intent | rest after a round | source |
+|---|---|---|
+| strength, heavy multi-joint | **2-3 min**, up to 5 | [3], [5] |
+| hypertrophy, multi-joint | **at least 2 min**; 60 s costs volume | [5], [6] |
+| hypertrophy, isolation | **60-90 s** | [6] |
+| conditioning, timed work | as short as the work allows | [7] |
+
+A superset of antagonists rests after the pair, not between the two: each muscle rests while the other works [8].
+
+> **Yours:** whether these replace the reference bands in TRAINER.md and `PLANNING`, or sit beside them.
+
+## Which structure
+
+| structure | reach for it when | not when |
+|---|---|---|
+| **straight sets** | the main lift of the workout; strength; any exercise that needs setup or a heavy load | — |
+| **superset** | two exercises for **opposing** muscles or unrelated groups (push/pull, biceps/triceps, upper/lower): saves time at little cost to either [8] | both exercises train the same muscle: the second is done fatigued, and the pair is one long set |
+| **circuit** | the goal is conditioning, or every exercise is light accessory or core work, and each station trains a **different** muscle [7] | it holds two exercises for the same muscle; it holds the workout's main work; it is chosen because it is cheap |
+
+**Time is not a reason to choose a shape.** A circuit pays one rest per round instead of one per exercise, so it always fits a budget more easily. When there is time left, spend it on working sets of the exercises already in the plan before adding exercises to a block.
+
+**What the first real plan got wrong** (ticket 11.2): four push-up variants in one circuit at 8 reps is chest working with no rest and no set near failure, then five triceps exercises the same way. Against this page: too many variants, a circuit of one muscle, sets far from failure, 20 minutes left unspent.
+
+## Order
+
+1. Multi-joint before single-joint; the exercise that matters most to the goal first [3].
+2. Within a group, the hardest pattern first.
+3. Core and timed work last, unless they are the point of the workout.
+
+## Sources
+
+| # | reference |
+|---|---|
